Part 3:
1. Design a circuit to compare two 4-bit binary words (e.g., Word 1: 0110, Word 2:
1010).
2. Use XNOR gates to compare each pair of corresponding bits from the two binary
words.
3. Combine the outputs of the XNOR gates using an AND gate to produce a single
signal indicating if the two words are equal.
4. Test the circuit with various 4-bit inputs to confirm its accuracy
 
























Using 4 XNOR gates with 2 inputs going to each one: A, A1 and so on, this created 4 outputs which went into two and gates. After the two and gates, the two outputs went into an AND, simulating that all inputs have to be  true or false (XNOR) to get the input high. However, if one paired letter like A and A1 were not matched in the same switched position, the output would always be low.
I came across a limitation, as I didn’t gave a 4 way AND, as this would have made the circuit simpler and clearer.





	

